<TopicPage slug="subjects/computer-science/expert/compilers-and-languages">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    By the expert tier you can write algorithms, trace them, and read two or three languages —
    but every program you have ever run had to pass through the same narrowing funnel: from
    text you can read, to instructions silicon can execute. This chapter is about that funnel
    and about the design decisions that make one language feel utterly different from another.
    Two syllabus topics anchor it: <strong>types of software and interrupts</strong> (how the
    operating system earns its privileged position, and how hardware grabs the CPU's attention)
    and <strong>types of programming language, translators and IDEs</strong> (what a compiler
    actually does to your code, and why language design is a series of trade-offs rather than
    progress towards one perfect language).
  </p>
  <p>
    The unifying idea is <strong>translation with verification</strong>. Every layer between
    your intention and the machine is a translator that also checks something: the lexer checks
    that words are well-formed, the parser checks that sentences are grammatical, the semantic
    analyser checks that they mean something, and the operating system enforces that the
    translated result behaves itself at run time. Layers exist because each check is cheaper
    and clearer at its own level.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="the five stages between your text file and running silicon" widget="equationStep" config={compCfg} />

  <h2>Inside the pipeline: lexing, parsing and semantic analysis</h2>
  <p>
    Watch the pipeline work on one line of a made-up language: <code>total = price + 4 * 2</code>.
    The <strong>lexer</strong> groups characters into tokens: identifier <code>total</code>,
    operator <code>=</code>, identifier <code>price</code>, operator <code>+</code>, number
    <code>4</code>, operator <code>*</code>, number <code>2</code> — seven tokens, with
    whitespace discarded. The lexer also classifies: it knows <code>total</code> is an
    identifier because it starts with a letter, and <code>4</code> is a number because it is
    all digits. This is deliberately mechanical — a lexer can be built from <strong>regular
    expressions</strong>, the simplest class of patterns, which is why it runs in a single
    fast pass.
  </p>
  <p>
    The <strong>parser</strong> then asks whether the token stream is a grammatical sentence
    and builds the abstract syntax tree (AST): an assignment node whose left child is
    <code>total</code> and whose right child is an addition node, whose second child is a
    multiplication node holding <code>4</code> and <code>2</code>. The tree shape is where
    meaning lives: <code>price + 4 * 2</code> parses as <code>price + (4 * 2)</code> because
    the grammar gives <code>*</code> higher precedence than <code>+</code>. Change the grammar
    and you change the language — operator precedence is not a law of mathematics, it is a
    choice written into the parsing rules. Syntax errors ("missing semicolon") are parser
    complaints: the tokens arrive in an order the grammar forbids.
  </p>
  <p>
    The <strong>semantic analyser</strong> asks the question the tree cannot answer alone: does
    this sentence mean anything? It walks the AST carrying a <strong>symbol table</strong> — the
    running list of every declared name, its type and its scope. Was <code>price</code> ever
    declared? Is a string being added to a number? A program can be perfectly grammatical and
    still meaningless, exactly as "colourless green ideas sleep furiously" is an English
    sentence that means nothing. Only after all three checks pass does optimisation begin —
    and every optimisation must be <em>provably equivalent</em>: constant folding rewrites
    <code>4 * 2</code> to <code>8</code> because the operands are literals that can never
    change; dead-code elimination deletes statements whose results can never be read.
  </p>
  <p>
    <em>Common misconception:</em> a compiler translates your code line by line, like a human
    translator reading aloud. It does not. A compiler reads the <strong>whole</strong> program
    (or whole unit) first, so it can report many errors in one pass and see enough context to
    optimise — it knows that a variable is never used again and that two loops can be merged.
    Line-by-line translation is the interpreter's job, and it explains the interpreter's
    signature behaviour: it stops at the <em>first</em> error and re-translates the same lines
    every time you run.
  </p>

  <h2>Types of software and interrupts (4.1)</h2>
  <p>
    Software divides into two families. <strong>Application software</strong> does the task the
    user bought the computer for: word processor, browser, spreadsheet, game.
    <strong>System software</strong> exists so applications can run: the operating system
    (OS), utility programs, and device drivers. The OS is the referee with root privileges, and
    its job list is worth memorising because every one of its duties is a response to a real
    problem: <strong>memory management</strong> allocates RAM to processes and keeps them from
    trampling each other; <strong>process management</strong> schedules the CPU among competing
    programs; <strong>file management</strong> turns raw disk blocks into named files and
    folders with permissions; <strong>I/O and device management</strong> hides messy hardware
    behind uniform drivers; <strong>security</strong> authenticates users and enforces access
    rights. <strong>Utility programs</strong> are the maintenance crew — disk formatter,
    defragmenter, backup tool, file compression, antivirus — system software that serves the
    user directly rather than serving other programs.
  </p>
  <p>
    Beneath the OS sits <strong>firmware</strong>: code stored in non-volatile memory on the
    motherboard. Power on, and the CPU executes it: the firmware runs the power-on self-test,
    finds the boot device, loads the <strong>bootloader</strong> from the disk's boot sector,
    and the bootloader loads the OS kernel into RAM and hands over. This chain — firmware to
    bootloader to kernel — is why a computer can start with literally nothing useful in RAM:
    each stage is a small program whose only job is to load the next, bigger one.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Interrupts</strong> solve the problem of how hardware gets the CPU's attention.
    The naive alternative is <strong>polling</strong>: the CPU repeatedly asks each device
    "anything for me?". A 3 GHz CPU checking a keyboard 100 times a second would burn hundreds
    of millions of idle cycles — yet a keyboard is pressed perhaps ten times a minute. Instead,
    when an event occurs the device raises an <strong>interrupt signal</strong>, and the CPU,
    between machine instructions, suspends the current program, pushes its context (program
    counter and key registers) onto the stack, looks up the address of the right
    <strong>interrupt service routine (ISR)</strong> in the <strong>interrupt vector table</strong>,
    runs it, then restores the saved context and resumes as if nothing happened. Interrupts
    come from hardware (keyboard, disk-completion, the <strong>timer interrupt</strong> that
    fires thousands of times a second and gives the scheduler its heartbeat) and from software
    (a division-by-zero trap, a system call asking the OS for a service). The vector table
    makes dispatch a table lookup, not a search; priority levels decide who wins when two
    devices interrupt simultaneously; and <strong>masking</strong> lets the CPU ignore
    low-priority interrupts during critical sections.
  </p>
  <p>
    <em>Common misconception:</em> the operating system runs "underneath" your programs,
    continuously. In fact the OS mostly does not run at all: your word processor has the CPU,
    and the OS only snaps into existence when an interrupt or system call hands control to it.
    Multitasking feels simultaneous because the timer interrupt slices time so finely — a
    scheduler deciding 1,000 times a second among a dozen processes — that no single program
    ever notices the gaps.
  </p>

  <h2>Programming languages, translators and IDEs (4.2)</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>Machine code</strong> is the CPU's native diet: binary patterns where each
    instruction means one microscopic operation. <strong>Assembly language</strong> is machine
    code with human-readable mnemonics, one-to-one. Adding two numbers in a teaching assembly
    looks like <code>LDA num1</code>, <code>ADD num2</code>, <code>STO result</code> — three
    instructions, one per thought, tied to that exact CPU. <strong>High-level languages</strong>
    (Python, Java, C) express the same intent as <code>result = num1 + num2</code> — one line,
    machine-independent, readable. The trade is real in both directions: high-level code is
    shorter, safer and portable; assembly gives precise control and minimum overhead, which is
    why it survives in device drivers, bootloaders and the hot inner loops of real-time
    systems.
  </p>
  <p>
    Three translators connect the levels. The <strong>assembler</strong> converts assembly to
    machine code essentially one-for-one — fast, and unforgiving. The <strong>compiler</strong>
    translates a whole high-level program to machine code (or another target) before it runs,
    listing all syntax errors it found; the resulting executable then runs at full speed with
    no translator present. The <strong>interpreter</strong> reads and executes high-level code
    a statement at a time, stopping at the first error — slower, because translation repeats
    every run, but unbeatable for instant feedback and for running the same code on different
    machines. Real systems blur the categories: Java compiles to <strong>bytecode</strong>, a
    portable fake machine code, which the Java Virtual Machine then executes (and, these days,
    compiles again to real machine code while running). Python is likewise compiled to bytecode
    and interpreted by a virtual machine. There is no clean line between "compiled" and
    "interpreted" — only points along a spectrum of how much is translated in advance.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Why languages differ</strong> is the same story told at design time. Static typing
    (C, Rust, TypeScript) checks types before running — earlier error catching, more
    optimisation fuel; dynamic typing (Python, JavaScript) checks at run time — faster to
    write, errors surface later. Memory management is another fork: manual (C — fast,
    dangerous), garbage-collected (Java, Go — safe, with occasional pauses), ownership
    (Rust — safe and fast, harder to learn). Paradigms — imperative, object-oriented,
    functional — are different theories of what a program <em>is</em>: a sequence of
    instructions, a web of objects sending messages, or a pipeline of functions producing new
    values from old. The explosion of languages is not indecision; it is different communities
    choosing different points in this trade space.
  </p>
  <p>
    An <strong>integrated development environment (IDE)</strong> bundles the toolchain into one
    program: a <strong>code editor</strong> with syntax highlighting, autocompletion and
    indentation guides; <strong>error diagnostics</strong> that underline mistakes as you type;
    integrated <strong>translators</strong> and a <strong>run-time environment</strong> (build
    and run buttons); <strong>auto-documentation</strong> of functions; and a
    <strong>debugger</strong> that can pause execution at a <strong>breakpoint</strong>, step
    through line by line, and display the live values of variables. The debugger matters more
    than beginners expect: it converts "it doesn't work" into a precise, observable question
    about state at a known moment.
  </p>
  <p>
    <em>Common misconception:</em> compiled programs are always faster than interpreted ones.
    Historically true on average, but modern just-in-time (JIT) systems watch which code runs
    hot, compile <em>just</em> those parts to machine code with assumptions taken from actual
    runtime behaviour, and outperform naive ahead-of-time compilation. JavaScript engines
    render modern 3D games at 60 frames a second — in what was once the slowest language class
    in common use.
  </p>

  <h2>Going deeper</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>Grammars have a formal hierarchy.</strong> Lexers work with regular languages —
    recognisable by finite automata with no memory beyond the current state. Parsers need
    context-free grammars, recognisable with a stack, and the jump is essential: counting
    matched brackets ("open two, close two") is impossible for any finite automaton, which is
    why nested structures cannot be lexed, only parsed. Ambiguity is a real grammatical
    property with real costs: the classic <em>dangling else</em> — does an <code>else</code>
    bind to the nearest or the outermost <code>if</code>? — is resolved by convention in most
    languages precisely because the grammar alone cannot decide. Language designers must
    choose grammars that parse efficiently (in linear time) or pay in compile times.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Optimisation is mathematics, not magic.</strong> After semantic analysis, serious
    compilers convert the AST into an intermediate representation in <em>single static
    assignment</em> (SSA) form — every variable is assigned exactly once, which makes data flow
    explicit. Register allocation then becomes graph colouring: variables whose live ranges
    overlap cannot share a CPU register, so the compiler colours a conflict graph; finding the
    minimum number of colours is NP-hard, so compilers use careful heuristics. The correctness
    bar is absolute: an optimisation that changes observable behaviour is a bug, and compiler
    bugs are uniquely vicious because they corrupt every program built with the tool. That is
    why the CompCert project — a C compiler with a machine-checked proof that its generated
    code is semantically equivalent to its input — is a landmark: it turned "compilers are
    usually right" into a theorem.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Open questions.</strong> Can JIT compilation use machine learning to predict which
    code will run hot before it does? Will memory-safe systems languages (Rust's borrow
    checker, and safer dialects of C++) displace C in operating-system kernels — a shift now
    visibly under way as Rust support lands in Linux and Windows components? And as large
    language models generate an increasing share of code, what does the compiler owe a
    reader-human versus a writer-machine? The pipeline built in 1957 for FORTRAN is still the
    spine of the answer, but its guarantees are being renegotiated for a world where code is
    written faster than it can be reviewed.
  </p>

  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> Aho, Lam, Sethi &amp; Ullman, <em>Compilers: Principles, Techniques and Tools</em> ("the dragon book"); Backus, "The FORTRAN Automatic Coding System" (1957); Cambridge IGCSE Computer Science 0478 syllabus, section 4; Leroy et al., the CompCert verified compiler.</p>
